NEW  <p> The article's wording is found in a newspaper article of 2000, as a web page that the article cites reproduces it.
NEW  <p> The article already cites a web page that its citation calls a 964-word excerpt from a book of 1981, William Lee Bradley's Siam Then (the reference named "Montes-Bradley", on the sentence about the King's death); I have since opened the archived copy that the citation gives (the Internet Archive's capture of 10 February 2015).
NEW  <p> It is a page of family history, headed "Descendants of Danyell Broadley de West Morton", the title the citation gives.
NEW  <p> In its entry for the missionary Dan Beach Bradley it carries, in quotation marks, a passage of some 900 words whose first line is "King Mongkut's prediction surpassed those of European scientists.
OLD  <p> The page itself attributes the passage at the end, in square brackets as it does three other passages, to "L.
OLD  <p> Robert Morris, National Post, Toronto, Ontario, Canada, 22 December 2000"; only after that, in a paragraph of its own, does it say "See Bradley, W.L., 'Siam Then ...'", and it names the book nowhere else.
OLD  <p> In that passage stands: "In fact, his calculations were better -- by about two seconds -- than those of the now exhausted French astronomers, who acknowledged his accuracy.
OLD  <p> The article's sentence is this one without "In fact" and "now exhausted".
NEW  <p> So the wording of the article's sentence is found in a newspaper article of 2000, as that page reproduces it; it is not in the 2021 paper, and it is not the wording of Bradley's sentence as Supavit quotes it (Bradley's sentence, and Supavit's article that quotes it, are under "Where to start" below).
OLD  <p> Nothing on the page says that the passage is an excerpt from Bradley's book.
OLD  <p> I have read one revision of the article and not its history, so I cannot say how the sentence came into it.
NEW  <p> I have not seen the newspaper itself, and the web page does not say what the newspaper's writer rested on; the passage does quote twice from the diary of "the Rev.
OLD  <p> Dan Beach Bradley", and the sentence with the two seconds is outside both quotations, in the writer's own words.
OLD  <p> 3.
NEW  <p> The reference for the date of the King's death does not fit it.
NEW  <p> The article cites that same web page for the King's death "on 1 October 1868", and its passage says "He died on his birthday, Oct. 18, 1868.
OLD  <p> The article's date is the one in the 2021 paper, "He died on 1 October 1868" (page 394), so it is the reference that does not fit the sentence.
NEW  <p> Where to start: Supavit Thavaraputta's article, "The 1868 Solar Eclipse: Some Considerations about Its Sources and Historiography", Thammasat Journal of History 8(2), 2021, pages 34 to 57 (its page at the journal, in Thai), is a research article on the Thai sources for this eclipse and on how its history has been written; it takes up the question of the two seconds on pages 46 to 48 and would be the place to start.
OLD  <p> It is in Thai, with an English abstract.
NEW  <p> It was read by Catalpa from the page images and by a fresh reader of Catalpa's; a fresh reader of mine read pages 45 to 48 and 50; the English quotation I have checked in the file's text.
NEW  <p> It quotes a source that does have the two seconds, William Lee Bradley's book of 1981 (page 149), and that book has them as something reported: "It was reported that he was greatly gratified to find that he was more correct by two seconds in his calculations than were the French astronomers".
NEW  <p> I have not seen Bradley's book.
NEW  <p> The missionary Dan Beach Bradley (1804–1873), in whose entry the web page of point 2 carries its passage, is another man: the book of 1981 is by a later Bradley, and nothing I have read says whose sentence it is or when it was written.
NEW  <p> What else I have not read: For the French expedition the paper refers to its authors' chapter of 2017, which none of us has read.
NEW  <p> Aubin's chapter of 2010, which the article also cites, may have the two seconds too: its draft, as Catalpa reports it, has them as the King's own boast (the background is in the full note below).
NEW  <p> ↑ Back to the list of notes
